WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.480
Welcome back to
learning how to learn!

2
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.640
In this video, we'll explore

3
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.120
some exciting new research
on generative AI,

4
00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:14.540
as well as how our brains form

5
00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:18.100
connections when we
learn, AND how we can

6
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.080
use the power of
metaphors to help

7
00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.860
us understand and
remember new information.

8
00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:27.660
Remember how we've
talked about chunks,

9
00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:30.180
those compact packages of

10
00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:34.015
information that your
mind can easily access?

11
00:00:34.015 --> 00:00:37.370
We've symbolized those
chunks as ribbons.

12
00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:40.790
Let's dive a little deeper
so we can see how this

13
00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:44.770
relates to what's actually
going on in our brain.

14
00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:49.030
Our neurons—tiny
biological cells—

15
00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:53.445
are fundamental building blocks
of learning in the brain.

16
00:00:53.445 --> 00:00:58.100
We make connections between
neurons as we're learning.

17
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:01.280
The clumps of connected
neurons as we

18
00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:04.760
learn something form
what we call "chunks."

19
00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.000
The connections we make between

20
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.700
neurons strengthen
with practice,

21
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:12.560
forming neural
pathways that help

22
00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.965
us recall information
more easily.

23
00:01:15.965 --> 00:01:19.480
Think of it like
links in a necklace.

24
00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:22.160
Each time you practice or

25
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:25.560
retrieve the information
from your own brain,

26
00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:29.510
the links in the neural
necklace become stronger.

27
00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:33.100
Metaphors, as it turns out,

28
00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:36.420
are not just a literary device.

29
00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:41.140
They play a crucial role
in how we understand and

30
00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:43.560
process new information by

31
00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:46.675
linking it to information
we already know.

32
00:01:46.675 --> 00:01:49.870
By linking these
abstract concepts to

33
00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:54.610
the more concrete, familiar
ideas already in our minds,

34
00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:57.630
metaphors can help
our brains form

35
00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:01.050
connections and make
sense of complex topics.

36
00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:08.330
This is where generative
AI—like ChatGPT—comes in.

37
00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:10.930
The flow of information

38
00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:14.230
through the transformers
that are at the heart of

39
00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:17.670
these AI platforms
are surprisingly like

40
00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:22.170
the flow of information
through the human brain.

41
00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:27.350
These powerful language models
can help us create vivid,

42
00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:30.850
memorable metaphors to
explain challenging concepts.

43
00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:36.310
For example, when asked to
explain the difference between

44
00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:41.485
"main guard" and "scope"
in Python programming,

45
00:02:41.485 --> 00:02:44.630
ChatGPT generated this metaphor:

46
00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:46.890
"Think of the main guard in

47
00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:51.130
a Python script as the
gatekeeper of a castle,

48
00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:55.780
and scope as the various
rooms within that castle."

49
00:02:55.780 --> 00:03:00.000
By relating these abstract
programming concepts

50
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.300
to a familiar image of a castle,

51
00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:07.280
the metaphor helps us
understand and remember

52
00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:09.800
the difference
between main guard

53
00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:11.300
and scope more effectively.

54
00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:17.120
What can be surprising is
that generative AI can

55
00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.820
devise many different metaphors

56
00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:23.035
that can be surprisingly
different from one another,

57
00:03:23.035 --> 00:03:27.950
even while each metaphor
is useful in its own way.

58
00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:32.810
You can almost think
of metaphors as tents,

59
00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:35.290
where the tent poles pull

60
00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:37.930
the tent up is
where the metaphor

61
00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:40.230
is most powerfully similar

62
00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:43.550
to the underlying
concept it's related to.

63
00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:48.760
Different metaphors hold the
tent up at different places.

64
00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:51.830
For example, if you're
trying to understand

65
00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:56.390
the structure of atoms with
their nucleus and electrons,

66
00:03:56.390 --> 00:03:58.570
you could use the metaphor that

67
00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:01.505
atoms are similar to a school.

68
00:04:01.505 --> 00:04:05.460
The nucleus is like the
principal's office,

69
00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:09.920
central to the school's
functioning and decision-making.

70
00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:11.920
The electrons are like

71
00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:15.400
students occupying
different classrooms,

72
00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.815
that is—energy levels—

73
00:04:17.815 --> 00:04:19.920
and moving between them.

74
00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:23.700
That gives two points
of commonality,

75
00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:27.820
two tent poles in
our tent metaphor.

76
00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:31.340
You can see, though,
that this metaphor is

77
00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:34.485
also a poor fit in many places.

78
00:04:35.485 --> 00:04:41.290
Or we could say that atoms
are like peaches or mangoes.

79
00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:44.750
The nucleus is like a
pit at the center of

80
00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:49.315
the fruit containing
most of the atom's mass.

81
00:04:49.315 --> 00:04:52.190
Second, electrons
are like the pulp

82
00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:55.360
of the fruit surrounding
the nucleus.

83
00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:58.150
Third, the electron shells

84
00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:00.535
are like the layers
of the fruit,

85
00:05:00.535 --> 00:05:03.250
that is, skin, flesh,

86
00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:07.850
and core, representing
different energy levels.

87
00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:10.710
That's three points
of commonality

88
00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:13.530
for this metaphor but obviously,

89
00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:17.150
there's a lot of information
this metaphor misses.

90
00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:23.290
Another metaphor for atoms
is the planetary system.

91
00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:27.050
This also has three
points of commonality,

92
00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:28.790
but those points are very

93
00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:31.315
different from a peach or mango.

94
00:05:31.315 --> 00:05:34.905
The nucleus is like
the sun at the center,

95
00:05:34.905 --> 00:05:38.090
electrons orbit
around the nucleus

96
00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:40.980
like planets orbiting the sun.

97
00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:44.110
Different energy levels are

98
00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:48.130
different orbital
distances of planets.

99
00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:51.385
Those are places of good fit,

100
00:05:51.385 --> 00:05:55.065
but clearly, there are
places of poor fit—

101
00:05:55.065 --> 00:05:58.950
that is, the fuzzy quantum nature

102
00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:02.190
of electrons and the
nucleus, for example.

103
00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:05.350
We could instead replace

104
00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:09.410
the solar system with
swirling clouds.

105
00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:12.450
This new metaphor
captures part of

106
00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:15.250
the underlying
concept of an atom in

107
00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:17.690
a different way that illustrates

108
00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:21.350
the fuzzy quantum
nature of the atom.

109
00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:24.530
There are other metaphors
that could have

110
00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:27.690
had even more points
of commonality.

111
00:06:27.690 --> 00:06:32.690
For example, a city is an
interesting metaphor for atoms.

112
00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:35.450
Among other things, the city has

113
00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:37.645
bridges that are
like chemical bonds.

114
00:06:38.645 --> 00:06:42.830
The next time you're struggling
to grasp a new concept,

115
00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:46.750
try creating a metaphor
through generative AI

116
00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:51.720
to help your brain form
those crucial connections.

117
00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:53.910
Prompt it to give
you a metaphor for

118
00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:56.370
a concept you might
be struggling to

119
00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:58.690
understand. And don't hesitate

120
00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:01.830
to ask for another
metaphor if you'd like.

121
00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:04.530
It can be surprising
what kind of

122
00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:08.510
wildly different
metaphors it can provide.

123
00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:11.170
Perhaps ask it for
a metaphor that

124
00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:13.110
relates to your favorite hobby.

125
00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:17.970
That's all for now—
Happy learning!